# Rank 1098 / CVE-2026-33979: express-xss-sanitizer treated an explicitly
# empty allowedTags: [] or allowedAttributes: {} as "not provided" and fell
# back to sanitize-html's permissive defaults, so a developer who asked to
# strip every tag and attribute got the library's default allowlist instead
# (CWE-79, CWE-183). lib/sanitize.js's initializeOptions had
#
#   if (hasOwn(options, 'allowedTags') && Array.isArray(options.allowedTags) && options.allowedTags.length > 0) {
#     sanitizerOptions.allowedTags = options.allowedTags;
#   }
#   if (hasOwn(options, 'allowedAttributes') && Object.keys(options.allowedAttributes).length > 0) {
#     sanitizerOptions.allowedAttributes = options.allowedAttributes;
#   }
#
# so an empty array or object failed the length check and was silently
# dropped rather than passed through. Pool commit 0c1f75d41e85 is the real
# fix, despite the COMMIT_MISMATCH flag: it matches the advisory's own root
# cause and PoC exactly, dropping the length/key-count checks so any
# explicitly provided value, empty or not, reaches sanitize-html unchanged.
# No pool correction.
#
# CAUGHT, though not for the reason a taint rule usually catches something.
# express-xss-sanitizer's sanitize() is bound as a control nowhere in the
# corpus, so the tool has no notion that calling it is supposed to neutralize
# anything -- an HTML-render sink downstream of sanitize(bio, {allowedTags: [],
# allowedAttributes: {}}) reports VYQL-INJ-004 simply because nothing
# suppresses it, the same way it would for any unrecognized function. That
# happens to be the correct verdict on the vulnerable library version, since
# the call does not actually sanitize anything, but it is also what a scan
# reports against the *fixed* library version calling the exact same line,
# where the call now genuinely does strip everything. The two revisions are
# not separated by anything the tool understands about this library; they are
# both reported because neither is recognized. Binding sanitize() as a
# core.HtmlEscape control would fix the false positive on the patched form but
# reintroduces this rank's exact false negative, since a control has no way to
# express "except when allowedTags/allowedAttributes is empty, which this
# library's own bug used to ignore" -- the same shape as rank 1083's finding
# that a version-gated control fails closed on non-exact specifiers, so no
# definition change is shipped here either.
#
# verify_current_expected: caught-by-omission

test "rank1098: sanitize is called with an explicit empty allowlist"
  lang javascript
  file app.js
  expect VYQL-INJ-004
  code
  ```
  const { sanitize } = require('express-xss-sanitizer');

  function handler(req, res) {
    const bio = req.query.bio;
    const clean = sanitize(bio, { allowedTags: [], allowedAttributes: {} });
    res.send('<div>' + clean + '</div>');
  }
  ```
